<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">194617</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2025539</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Properties of a cell line from human adenocarcinoma of the rectum.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Watkins</surname><given-names>J. F.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sanger</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>6</month><year>1977</year></pub-date><volume>35</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>785</fpage><lpage>794</lpage><abstract><p>A new, highly differentiated line of cells derived from adenocarcinoma of the rectum (HT55) is described. This line is noteworthy for the following features: 1. The role played in its development by the use of UV-inactivated Sendai virus to attach tumour cell clumps to plastic bottles. 2. Evidence that it produces RNA-containing material of density 1-5--1-16 g/ml. 3. Induction of bone formation in the stroma when grown in athymic mice. 4. Stimulation of primary CBA mouse embryo fibroblasts to form a transient nodule when mixed with them and injected into adult CBA mice. The karyotype and growth-cycle characteristics of the line are described.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>Fig. 1</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0079-a" xlink:role="787"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>Fig. 2</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0079-b" xlink:role="787"/></fig><fig id="F3"><label>Fig. 3</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0080-a" xlink:role="788"/></fig><fig id="F4"><label>Fig. 4</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0081-a" xlink:role="789"/></fig><fig id="F5"><label>Fig. 5</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0082-a" xlink:role="790"/></fig><fig id="F6"><label>Fig. 6</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0082-b" xlink:role="790"/></fig><fig id="F7"><label>Fig. 7</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0083-a" xlink:role="791"/></fig><fig id="F8"><label>Fig. 8</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0083-b" xlink:role="791"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0077.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="785" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0078.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="786" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0079.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="787" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0080.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="788" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0081.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="789" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0082.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="790" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0083.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="791" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0084.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="792" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0085.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="793" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00303-0086.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="794" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>